// plain.zm -- the look the control set is read in: the plainest thing that is legible.
//
// **Not a theme and not a design.** F277 says in as many words that pixel parity with a platform's
// widgets is not a goal, so this is the legible default and the thing an application replaces. It
// is deliberately the smallest answer to each of the six decisions in `theme.zm`'s header --
// square, filled, unedged, one grey, one size of padding -- because an application replacing it
// should not have to undo anything first. `modern.zm` is the demonstration of what the vocabulary
// can say; this is the reference.
//
// **Since F295 it is two seeds and a short list** (D8). What used to be twenty colours written out
// is `derive` over a neutral seed and an accent seed, and the list below it is the measurement of
// whether the derivation is right: one override, and it is not a colour.

// The neutral a plain window is built from. **Barely a hue at all** -- a hundredth of chroma at the
// blue end, which is the difference between a grey that belongs to its accent and one that sits
// beside it.
fn plain_seeds() -> Seeds {
    return Seeds {
        neutral: paint.oklch(0.5, 0.010, 286.0),
        // **The blue the gallery was hard-coding into its own focus ring**, which is what naming
        // the token was for: the one place the identity colour lived was a call site in an
        // application.
        accent: paint.oklch(0.55, 0.188, 261.0),
        // A face is darker than the window it sits on, which is what "flat grey on near-white"
        // means and what this look has always done. See `Toward`.
        surfaces: Toward.Ink,
        edged: false
    }
}

pub fn plain() -> controls.Style {
    // **One override, and it is not a colour.** The marks have to be characters the face actually
    // has: `lib/ui` defaults to triangles, and an application whose font lacks U+25B8 says so here.
    // Every colour in this look is a step of one of two ramps.
    return derive(plain_seeds(), Scheme.Light)
}

// The same look on a dark window.
//
// **It used to be a list of overrides that had to discover four things an inversion gets wrong** --
// faces lighter than the window, a white hover veil, `on_accent` flipping, a deeper shadow. Every
// one of them is what a step *means* on a ramp read from the other end, so the whole of it is now
// the second argument. That is D8's claim about dark falling out mechanically, and this file is
// what it cost: one word.
pub fn plain_dark() -> controls.Style {
    return derive(plain_seeds(), Scheme.Dark)
}
